@version 0.01

@h1{Interactive Beam Deflection Diagram}

@p{
This is a cantilever beam, fixed at the wall on the left and free at the
right end. The point load P acts at the free end. The deflection curve
is drawn as SVG and stays live: drag the load slider below (in the app
UI, via // @input target="deflection") to resample just this curve
without re-running the whole program.
}

P := 12;     // @input slider min=1 max=40 step=1 target="deflection"
P_max := 40; // slider's max value, used only to fix the plot's vertical scale
L := 10;
E := 29000;
I := 800;

// The deflection formula, written once. P is a parameter so the same formula
// serves both the live curve (at the current P) and the fixed vertical scale
// (evaluated at P_max, below), with no duplicated math.
deflection(P, x) := -P*x^2*(3*L-x)/(6*E*I);

svg(400, 175,
    line(10,55, 10,125),
    line(10,55, 0,65),
    line(10,71, 0,81),
    line(10,87, 0,97),
    line(10,103, 0,113),
    line(10,119, 0,129),
    line(10,90, 390,90),

    arrow(390,15, 390,85),
    text(385,10, "P"),
    curve(deflection(P, x), x, 0, L, 40, "deflection", deflection(P_max, L))
);
// curve()'s 7th arg fixes the vertical scale to the worst-case deflection (at
// P_max) so the curve visibly grows as P is dragged up, not auto-rescaled.

def_{max} := deflection(P, L);
eval(def_{max});
